.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt KVM-GROW 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kvm-grow
.Nd grow a running virtual machine's root disk, partition and filesystem
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar vm
.Ar GiB
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
makes the root disk of the running machine
.Ar vm
.Ar GiB
gibibytes, and makes the guest use all of it.
A larger disk alone is not enough: the partition table and the filesystem
inside still describe the old size, so a guest given 50G can still only
use 40G.
.Nm
grows all three layers, in order:
.Bl -enum
.It
The disk.
A disk on a ZFS volume, as every machine kldload makes has, is grown with
.Ql zfs set volsize .
A disk in a file is grown in the next step, by qemu, which holds the file
locked while the guest runs.
.It
The guest is told, with
.Ql virsh blockresize .
.It
Inside the guest, over
.Xr ssh 1
as root: the root partition is grown to the end of the disk with
.Xr growpart 1 ,
and the filesystem to fill the partition \(em
.Xr resize2fs 8
for ext2, ext3 and ext4,
.Xr xfs_growfs 8
for XFS,
.Ql btrfs filesystem resize
for Btrfs,
.Xr resize.f2fs 8
for F2FS.
Then the filesystem's size is compared with its size before: a run that
changed nothing succeeds only if the partition already reached the end of
the disk.
.El
.Pp
The first disk in the machine's definition is taken to be its root disk,
as it is on every image kldload ships.
.Pp
Each layer reports what it did.
Running it again is safe, and is how a run that stopped part-way is
finished: a layer already at the size says so and the next one carries on.
.Pp
The machine must be running, so its filesystem can be grown, and must have
an address this host can reach and log in to as root with its key, as
machines made by
.Xr kvm-clone 8
can.
Its address is found with
.Xr kldload-vm-ip 8 .
.Pp
.Nm
must run as root; run as a user it re-executes itself with
.Xr sudo 8
.Pq without a password prompt .
.Fl -help
needs neither.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar GiB
The new size, a whole number of gibibytes.
A size smaller than the disk is refused; the same size grows only the
layers inside that have not caught up.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit 0.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 1n -compact
.It 0
Grown, or every layer was already at full size.
.It 1
A layer failed, or the guest had no address or could not be logged in to;
the lines before say how far it got.
.It 2
Refused before changing anything \(em a usage error, a size that is not a
whole number or is smaller than the disk, a machine that is not running
\(em or the guest's root is on a layout the guest step does not grow; the
disk has been grown by then, and
.Nm
says so.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every output below was printed by
.Nm
on a kldload host on 2026-09-29, against
.Ql gr-3246078 ,
a throwaway clone of a Rocky Linux golden with a 40G disk and XFS on
.Pa /dev/vda4 ,
and
.Ql gr-3246078-f ,
a machine with a disk in a qcow2 file and no operating system; both were
deleted afterwards.
.Ss Growing
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-grow gr-3246078 44
block:      rpool/vms/gr-3246078 40G -> 44G
guest:      virsh blockresize gr-3246078 vda 44G
root:       /dev/vda4 (xfs) on /dev/vda partition 4
filesystem: 39G before
partition:  grown -- CHANGED: partition=4 start=2258944 old: size=81627103 end=83886046 new: size=90015711 end=92274654
            meta-data=/dev/vda4              isize=512    agcount=18, agsize=584767 blks
\&...
            data blocks changed from 10203387 to 11251963
filesystem: 43G after
RESULT: grown
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss Running it again
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-grow gr-3246078 44
block:      rpool/vms/gr-3246078 is already 44G
guest:      virsh blockresize gr-3246078 vda 44G
root:       /dev/vda4 (xfs) on /dev/vda partition 4
filesystem: 43G before
partition:  already at full size
            meta-data=/dev/vda4              isize=512    agcount=20, agsize=584767 blks
\&...
filesystem: 43G after
RESULT: already at full size
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Ss A disk in a file
The machine had no operating system, so there was nobody to log in to;
the disk itself was grown:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-grow gr-3246078-f 2
block:      /var/lib/libvirt/images/gr-3246078-f.qcow2 1G -> 2G (by blockresize)
guest:      virsh blockresize gr-3246078-f vda 2G
kvm-grow: gr-3246078-f has no address yet — the block device is grown; run again for the filesystem
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss A guest that cannot be logged in to
Here with
.Xr sshd 8
stopped inside the guest:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-grow gr-3246078 45
block:      rpool/vms/gr-3246078 44G -> 45G
guest:      virsh blockresize gr-3246078 vda 45G
ssh: connect to host 192.168.122.3 port 22: Connection refused
kvm-grow: could not ssh to root@192.168.122.3 -- the block device is 45G; run again for the filesystem
# (exited 1)
.Ed
.Ss What it refuses
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# kvm-grow gr-3246078 42
kvm-grow: refusing to shrink rpool/vms/gr-3246078 (44G -> 42G)
# (exited 2)
# kvm-grow gr-3246078 4.5
kvm-grow: size must be whole GiB, got '4.5'
# (exited 2)
# kvm-grow klab-golden-rocky 50
kvm-grow: klab-golden-rocky is not running — the guest has to be up to grow its filesystem
# (exited 2)
# kvm-grow web/1 50
kvm-grow: 'web/1' is not a VM name
# (exited 2)
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -diag
.It "kvm-grow: VM is not running \(em the guest has to be up to grow its filesystem"
Start it and run again.
.It "kvm-grow: refusing to shrink DISK (CUR -> NEW)"
Nothing was changed.
Shrinking a disk under a filesystem destroys what lies past the new end.
.It "kvm-grow: VM has no address yet \(em the block device is grown; run again for the filesystem"
.Xr kldload-vm-ip 8
found no address; a guest still booting has none yet.
.It "kvm-grow: could not ssh to root@ADDRESS -- the block device is SIZE; run again for the filesystem"
The guest refused the login or did not answer.
.It "partition:  growpart NOT INSTALLED on NAME -- install PACKAGE"
The guest lacks
.Xr growpart 1 ;
the package that has it on that distribution is named.
The filesystem is still grown as far as its partition allows.
.It "growroot: root is the LVM volume DEVICE: ..."
The root filesystem is on LVM, which is not grown; the message names the
commands.
The disk has been grown and the exit status is 2.
.It "growroot: root is DATASET (zfs), not a partition: grow its pool or volume by hand"
A guest with ZFS on root.
Grow its partition with
.Xr growpart 1 ,
then its pool with
.Ql zpool online -e .
.It "growroot: NOT grown -- the filesystem did not change size; see the lines above"
Every step ran and the filesystem is the size it was, though the
partition had not reached the end of the disk.
.It "kvm-grow: FAIL at line N: COMMAND"
A command on this host failed; it is named.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssh 1 ,
.Xr virsh 1 ,
.Xr growpart 1 ,
.Xr kldload-vm-ip 8 ,
.Xr kvm-clone 8 ,
.Xr kvm-create 8 ,
.Xr resize2fs 8 ,
.Xr xfs_growfs 8 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was ported from the grow verb of
.Xr vmxplore 1
on 2026-09-26.
.Pp
On 2026-09-29, writing this page: a machine with its disk in a file could
never be grown, since both
.Xr qemu-img 1
commands it ran were refused the running guest's lock; an unreachable
guest exited 255 instead of 1; and an LVM root failed with a message that
did not say it was LVM.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Only the root disk, and only the partition and filesystem mounted at
.Pa / ,
are grown.
A second disk, a separate
.Pa /home ,
LVM and ZFS inside the guest are left to be grown by hand.
.Pp
A disk cannot be shrunk.
.Pp
The guest's host key is not checked: machines made from goldens get a new
one each time they are made.
